NNAAMMEE
     uunnaammee — print operating system name

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     uunnaammee [--aammnnpprrssvv]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The uunnaammee utility writes strings representing one or more system charac‐
     teristics to the standard output.  The formats and meanings of all these
     strings vary among operating systems.

     The options are as follows:

     --aa      Behave as though all of the options --mmnnrrssvv were specified.

     --mm      Print the name of the hardware type.  On OpenBSD, this is the
             kernel architecture as returned by machine(1).

     --nn      Print the network name of this machine.  On OpenBSD, the same
             name can be printed with hostname(1).

     --pp      Print the application architecture as returned by arch(1) --ss.

     --rr      Print the operating system release.  On OpenBSD, the format is
             _d_i_g_i_t._d_i_g_i_t.

     --ss      Print the operating system name.  On OpenBSD, this is always
             “OpenBSD”.

     --vv      Print the operating system version.  On OpenBSD, the format is
             _S_Y_S_T_E_M_N_A_M_E#_b_u_i_l_d_n_u_m_b_e_r with the _S_Y_S_T_E_M_N_A_M_E mentioned in
             config(8).

     If no options are specified, uunnaammee prints the operating system name as if
     the --ss option had been specified.

     If --aa or more than one option is specified, OpenBSD prints the selected
     fields separated by single space characters in the following order: oper‐
     ating system name, network name, release, version, kernel architecture,
     application architecture.

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
     The uunnaammee utility exits 0 on success, and >0 if an error occurs.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     hostname(1), machine(1), uname(3)

SSTTAANNDDAARRDDSS
     The uunnaammee utility is compliant with the IEEE Std 1003.1-2008 (“POSIX.1”)
     specification.

     The flag [--pp] is an extension to that specification.

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The uunnaammee command first appeared in PWB/UNIX 1.0 and was reimplemented
     for 4.4BSD.

